      "text_raw": "The charge conservation\nequation is copied on top.\nAll voltages are now con-\nverted into z-transforms.\nOne delay corresponds to\nz−1.\nThe gain V /V is now\nout in\nreadily written. It shows\nthat the gain equals a, the\nratio of the two capacitors,\nmultiplied by a factor in z.\nTo see what this factor\nmeans in the frequency\ndomain, z is substituted by\nexp( jvT ). For frequencies\nc\nmuch lower than the clock\nfrequency f , this exponential can be developed into a power series, which can be cut off after jvT .\nc c",
      "text": "The charge conservation equation is copied on top. All voltages are now converted into z-transforms. One delay corresponds to z−1. The gain V /V is now out in readily written. It shows that the gain equals a, the ratio of the two capacitors, multiplied by a factor in z. To see what this factor means in the frequency domain, z is substituted by exp( jvT ). For frequencies c much lower than the clock frequency f , this exponential can be developed into a power series, which can be cut off after jvT . c c"
